00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:03.363
and failures

00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:04.831
to say nothing of India's success

00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:06.666
last month with their lunar lander.

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:08.234
So the pressure was really on hand

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:09.636
and I guess they pulled it off.

00:01:09.636 --> 00:01:11.071
So how successful is this?

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:12.038
What does this mean

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:13.373
for the Japanese space program

00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:16.309
and also for space travel in general?

00:01:16.309 --> 00:01:16.743
Sure.

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:18.478
Well, this this is a big deal for them.

00:01:18.478 --> 00:01:20.013
This is their first lunar probe

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:21.214
they're trying to launch.

00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:22.549
They've successfully launched it,

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:23.583
but they're not out of the woods.

00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:24.784
They still got to get it

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:26.886
to a soft landing on the moon.

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:28.688
So the main purpose of this mission

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:30.990
primarily is to actually soft

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:32.659
land successfully on the moon.

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:34.861
They do have some scientific instruments,

00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:36.896
but the main goal was to,

00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:37.564
you know,

00:01:37.564 --> 00:01:39.299
establish Japan as a lunar power,

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:40.500
if you will. Okay.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.468
And this is a twofer here.

00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:41.901
This mission,

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:43.470
there's a new X-ray telescope.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:44.537
There's also that lightweight,

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:45.605
high precision lander.

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:45.905
Which one do

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:46.773
you want to talk about first?

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:48.741
Which one?

