I'm sorry?

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:49.676
Which one were

00:01:49.676 --> 00:01:50.844
which one you want to talk about first?

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:52.779
The lander or the telescope?

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:54.047
Oh, well, either one.

00:01:54.047 --> 00:01:54.547
Okay.

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:56.883
Let's go back to the the the lander.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:58.485
Space.com says

00:01:58.485 --> 00:01:59.319
it will take a long

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:00.520
looping and fuel efficient

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:01.788
route to the moon,

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:02.989
finally reaching lunar orbit

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:04.524
three to four months from now.

00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:05.692
It'll be another month or so.

00:02:05.692 --> 00:02:07.994
Before attempting a touchdown.

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:09.129
And that's a big shift towards

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:10.697
being able to land where we want

00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:12.765
and not just where it is easy to land,

00:02:12.966 --> 00:02:15.068
as has been the case before.

00:02:15.068 --> 00:02:15.802
So, again,

00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:17.670
why is this so important

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:18.605
when it comes to

00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:20.273
not just exploring the moon,

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:22.642
but future exploration of other planets?

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:25.278
And just how difficult is this?

00:02:25.278 --> 00:02:26.713
What they're trying to do here?

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:28.548
Well, trying to

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:30.450
land on the moon is not a simple task.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:34.120
Only four countries have done so so far,

00:02:34.354 --> 00:02:36.055
and there have been multiple failures

00:02:36.055 --> 00:02:37.090
by others.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:38.591
And so anyway,

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:39.325
this is a big deal

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:40.026
for Japan

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:40.860
to establish

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.396
that they have the technology to do this.

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:43.796
The fact

00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:45.265
that they're taking a couple of months

00:02:45.265 --> 00:02:46.533
to get there shows

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:48.601
that they're conserving their fuel.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:49.202
But, you know,

00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:51.604
as you mentioned, the image based,

00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:53.439
you know, the data source

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:55.441
being image based is a big deal.

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:57.577
Because if you remember in Apollo 11,

00:02:57.911 --> 00:02:59.612
Neil Armstrong was coming down

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:01.748
under the autopilot and they were

00:03:02.148 --> 00:03:04.717
the autopilot was taking them to

00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:05.585
a big boulder.

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:07.086
And he had to take manual control

00:03:07.086 --> 00:03:08.388
and fly away from it.

00:03:08.388 --> 00:03:09.889
And so that's kind of what

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:11.157
this vision landing system

00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:11.958
is going to do.

00:03:11.958 --> 00:03:13.359
It's going to look for the obstructions

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:14.827
